Circagraph server for Arch Linux
================================

Install
-------

From the AUR, with an AUR helper or by hand:

    git clone https://aur.archlinux.org/circagraph-server.git
    cd circagraph-server && makepkg -si

It builds cg-convert and the pgmp extension for this system's PostgreSQL, and installs
the server, the `circagraph` system user (and `circagraph-logs`, the log importers') and its
services. As Arch packages do, it leaves PostgreSQL and the database to you; finish with:

    sudo circagraph-setup

which:

  - initializes PostgreSQL in /var/lib/postgres/data if it is not yet, and starts and
    enables it;
  - writes /etc/circagraph/circagraph.env, with a new AUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY;
  - creates the `circagraph` PostgreSQL role and database, with the pgmp extension;
  - migrates the database (circagraph-migrate) and starts circagraph.service,
    which listens on 127.0.0.1:5173.

It is safe to run again. The server runs on Arch's Node.js (`nodejs`, or an LTS package
that provides it).

PostgreSQL major upgrades: pgmp is built for the PostgreSQL this package was built with.
After pacman installs a new PostgreSQL major version, upgrade the cluster as the Arch
wiki describes (pg_upgrade), and rebuild this package so pgmp matches it.

From openchronology-server
--------------------------

Circagraph was called OpenChronology, and this package openchronology-server, until 0.30.
An AUR helper's upgrade moves an installation over: the last openchronology-server is an
empty package that depends on circagraph-server, which conflicts with the older ones, so
pacman asks

    :: circagraph-server and openchronology-server are in conflict. Remove openchronology-server? [y/N]

Answer y. To move by hand instead, install the new package and answer the same:

    yay -S circagraph-server

(or clone https://aur.archlinux.org/circagraph-server.git and `makepkg -si`). Removing
openchronology-server prints what its removal leaves and how to delete it: ignore that, as
nothing is deleted. Installing circagraph-server then, with circagraph-setup:

  - moves /etc/openchronology to /etc/circagraph, and openchronology.env to circagraph.env,
    leaving a link under each old name;
  - keeps the installation's system users (openchronology and openchronology-logs), its
    database and role (openchronology) and its state folders (/var/lib/openchronology-github):
    each service gets a drop-in, /etc/systemd/system/<service>.service.d/50-users.conf, naming
    them;
  - takes the upgrade path: the server stays stopped until you run `sudo circagraph-migrate`.

The services are circagraph, circagraph-mail, circagraph-syslog, circagraph-github and
circagraph-journald now; each also answers to its old name (openchronology-mail, …). Where
systemd runs, removing openchronology-server disabled its services, so enable again the ones
that ran besides the server (circagraph-migrate enables the server itself), for example
`sudo systemctl enable --now circagraph-mail`; where their links remain, setup enables them
again itself and says so. The commands are circagraph-setup, circagraph-migrate,
circagraph-reset-admin-password, cg-import-logs and cg-import-github; the old names
(openchronology-setup, openchronology-migrate, och-import-logs, …) still work.

pacman creates the circagraph and circagraph-logs users on every installation (sysusers.d
cannot depend on an installation's history); on one moved from openchronology-server they
stay unused, and you may delete them: `sudo userdel circagraph; sudo userdel circagraph-logs`.

The transitional openchronology-server may stay installed beside circagraph-server, or be
removed: `sudo pacman -R openchronology-server`. It will be retired from the AUR by a merge
request into circagraph-server.

Configure
---------

Edit /etc/circagraph/circagraph.env, then `sudo systemctl restart circagraph`:

  - APP_ORIGIN: the public HTTPS origin users reach, without a trailing slash. The server
    refuses a plain http:// origin other than localhost; on a trusted private network
    without a proxy, set ALLOW_INSECURE_ORIGIN=1 and HOST=0.0.0.0 to serve
    http://<name>:5173 directly (and open the port in your firewall). If the website reports "Server
    misconfigured", the reason is also in `journalctl -u circagraph`.
  - RESEND_API_KEY and AUTH_EMAIL_FROM for account email, then
    `sudo systemctl enable --now circagraph-mail`.
  - ADMIN_USERNAME, ADMIN_EMAIL and ADMIN_INITIAL_PASSWORD for the first administrator,
    created by `sudo circagraph-migrate`. The password is used once; to sign in with it
    again (or with a new one written there), run `sudo circagraph-reset-admin-password`.
  - Sign-in providers, human verification, quotas: see the comments in the file and
    authentication.md, administration.md in this directory.

Back up AUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY apart from the database: without it, enrolled authenticator
secrets cannot be decrypted. Do not change it once accounts exist.

Serve it publicly through an HTTPS reverse proxy. With Caddy:

    timescale.example {
        reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:5173
    }

Caddy 2.5 and later replace an incoming
X-Forwarded-For with the client address, so set TRUST_PROXY=1 (with another proxy, only if
it does the same; see README.md). Proxies should allow request bodies of at least 32 MiB
and streaming responses (live updates).

The syslog receiver (syslog.md) is configured in /etc/circagraph/syslog.env with routes
in /etc/circagraph/syslog-routes.json; then
`sudo systemctl enable --now circagraph-syslog`.

The GitHub receiver (github.md) mirrors issues and pull requests into timelines from
GitHub's webhooks, on 127.0.0.1:5580 behind your HTTPS reverse proxy. It is configured in
/etc/circagraph/github.env (the API key and the webhook's secret) with routes in
/etc/circagraph/github-routes.json; then
`sudo systemctl enable --now circagraph-github`.

The journald follower (journald.md) streams this machine's systemd journal, or another
machine's over SSH, into timelines. Configure it in /etc/circagraph/journald.env (the timeline or
routes, and which units); then `sudo systemctl enable --now circagraph-journald`.

The log importers run as their own system user, circagraph-logs, which cannot read
circagraph.env or log in to the database (#501); the journald follower has the
systemd-journal group as well. syslog.env, github.env and journald.env are
root:circagraph-logs, mode 0640, and so must be the files they name (routes, the
journald SSH key and known hosts): `sudo chown root:circagraph-logs FILE && sudo chmod
0640 FILE`. See syslog.md and journald.md, "Its own account". (On an installation from
openchronology-server, the users are openchronology and openchronology-logs.)

`cg-import-logs` imports historic log files (log-import.md), with OCH_API_URL and
OCH_API_KEY from the environment or syslog.env: `cg-import-logs --help`.
`cg-import-github` imports a repository's existing issues and pull requests (github.md),
with OCH_API_URL, OCH_API_KEY and GITHUB_TOKEN from the environment or github.env:
`cg-import-github --help`.

Upgrade
-------

Installing a newer package (from the AUR, `makepkg -si` again, or your AUR helper's upgrade)
stops the server and leaves the database and configuration as they are. It then lists:

  1. Settings this version's template has that /etc/circagraph/circagraph.env
     lacks. Compare with:
         diff -u /etc/circagraph/circagraph.env \
                 /usr/share/circagraph/circagraph.env.example
  2. A backup:  sudo -u postgres pg_dump circagraph > circagraph.sql
  3. sudo circagraph-migrate, which migrates the database and starts the server, and
     each of the mail worker (circagraph-mail) and the log importers
     (circagraph-syslog, circagraph-github, circagraph-journald) that is
     enabled. Disabled ones stay stopped.

circagraph-migrate is safe to run more than once. `--no-restart` migrates only and starts
nothing.

Upgrading from a version before #501 moves syslog.env, journald.env and the files they name
(SYSLOG_ROUTES, JOURNALD_ROUTES, JOURNALD_SSH_KEY, JOURNALD_KNOWN_HOSTS) from the
server's user to the log importers' (circagraph-logs), keeping their modes: only regular
files with one link, reached through no symbolic link, in folders only root can change. It
lists what it moved and what it left for you to change.

Remove
------

`sudo pacman -R circagraph-server` stops and removes the server. The configuration
(/etc/circagraph, including AUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY), the database and the system users
remain; delete them with (openchronology in place of circagraph for an installation from
openchronology-server, as removal says):

    sudo rm -rf /etc/circagraph /etc/openchronology /var/lib/circagraph /var/cache/circagraph \
      /etc/systemd/system/circagraph*.service.d
    sudo -u postgres dropdb circagraph
    sudo -u postgres dropuser circagraph
    sudo userdel circagraph
    sudo userdel circagraph-logs

Layout
------

  /usr/lib/circagraph                the application and cg-convert (node/bin/node is
                                     this system's /usr/bin/node)
  /usr/bin/circagraph-setup          first-time setup (run it once after installing)
  /usr/bin/circagraph-migrate        database migration
  /usr/bin/circagraph-reset-admin-password   the administrator's password, from the configuration
  /usr/bin/cg-import-logs            log file import
  /usr/bin/cg-import-github          GitHub import
  /usr/bin/openchronology-*, och-import-*   the same commands' names before 0.31
  /etc/circagraph                    configuration (created by circagraph-setup)
  /usr/share/circagraph              configuration templates of the installed version
  /usr/lib/systemd/system            circagraph, circagraph-mail, circagraph-syslog,
                                     circagraph-github, circagraph-journald
  /usr/lib/postgresql, /usr/share/postgresql/extension   pgmp for this system's PostgreSQL
